From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the Rigel IPL level asserted

INTF.IPL now belongs to the Rigel-backed Amiga interrupt domain.  It is a
publication of the level Rigel derives from INTREQ and INTENA, not a one-shot
host-interrupt latch.

Do not clear that publication when the CPU accepts an exception.  The source
remains asserted until classic hardware semantics change it, at which point
Bellatrix publishes the newly resolved level.
---
 src/ExecutionLoop.c | 22 ----------------------
 1 file changed, 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ExecutionLoop.c b/src/ExecutionLoop.c
index 5caefed..cd1d54c 100644
--- a/src/ExecutionLoop.c
+++ b/src/ExecutionLoop.c
@@ -443,28 +443,6 @@ void MainLoop()
                 /* Set SR */
                 setSR(SR);
 
-#if !defined(PISTORM_ANY_MODEL)
-                /*
-                 * The level has been consumed, so drop it.
-                 *
-                 * On PiStorm INTF.IPL mirrors a physical line that Paula holds
-                 * until the guest acknowledges, so clearing it here would be
-                 * wrong -- hence the guard. Standalone there is no line and no
-                 * Paula: the field is written by Emu68's own core-0 IRQ path,
-                 * once per host interrupt, and nothing else would ever lower
-                 * it. Leaving it set would re-fire the moment the guest's RTE
-                 * drops the SR mask, forever.
-                 *
-                 * Only when the level being taken is the one IPL asked for --
-                 * a higher-priority source (ARM_err at 7) must not swallow a
-                 * pending IPL.
-                 */
-                if (ctx->INTF.IPL && level == ctx->INTF.IPL)
-                {
-                    ctx->INTF.IPL = 0;
-                }
-#endif
-
                 /* Get VBR */
                 vbr = ctx->VBR;
 
